Gretchen,<br />
<br />
You asked for a count of how often the pre-2014 gap has actually come up an=
d how it was resolved. Eleven matters in the last four years across this of=
fice and Chicago.<br />
<br />
Three were resolved by restore from the retired examination platform. In ea=
ch the correspondence existed and the case system simply did not hold it. M=
edian turnaround two weeks.<br />
<br />
Two were resolved from the registrant's own copy. Both registrants had reta=
ined their examination correspondence under Rule 204-2 and produced it on r=
equest. This works and it costs nothing, but the record you get is the regi=
strant's record, and in a contested matter opposing counsel will say so.<br=
 />
<br />
One was resolved from FINRA's file on a joint broker-dealer cycle examinati=
on.<br />
<br />
Three were resolved by search of the electronic mail journal archive. Two p=
roduced the correspondence in full. The third produced a documented negativ=
e - the pleaded document did not exist - and I include that one in this inv=
entory deliberately. It was a resolution, not a failure.<br />
<br />
Two were accepted as gaps. In one, a FOIA request closed with a no records =
response and no appeal. In the other, the branch scoped the new examination=
 without the prior cycle's file and said so in the scoping memorandum.<br /=
>
<br />
The pattern is that the gap is almost never terminal and is very often mist=
aken for a fact about the underlying matter. In ten of the eleven, somebody=
's first written statement was some version of "the examination closed with=
 nothing required," and in seven of those ten that statement turned out to =
be wrong once somebody looked somewhere else.<br />
